::ENG2_U04_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Topic: public transportation. Which research question is focused enough for one assignment?{
~A. What is transportation?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and asks for a definition.
=B. How could later bus service affect after-school job access for high school students in one neighborhood?#Correct. This question is limited by service type, effect, audience, and place.
~C. Is transportation good?#Incorrect. This is too broad and opinion-based.
~D. Why is every bus system perfect?#Incorrect. This is biased and unrealistic.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A student writes, "School lunches." What should the student do next to create a research question?{
~A. Leave it as a complete research question.#Incorrect. It is only a topic.
~B. Remove the topic completely.#Incorrect. The topic can become useful after it is focused.
~C. Add an unrelated opinion about sports.#Incorrect. Research questions should stay on topic.
=D. Ask what specific issue, effect, or solution about school lunches will be researched.#Correct. A research question needs a focus.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is too broad?{
=A. How can schools improve everything for everyone?#Correct. This is too broad to answer in one assignment.
~B. How could a share table reduce unopened food waste in one school cafeteria?#Incorrect. This question is focused by solution and place.
~C. What effect could a school garden have on biology observations for tenth-grade students?#Incorrect. This question is focused.
~D. How could later library hours help students who work after school?#Incorrect. This is focused by solution and audience.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is too narrow for a research assignment?{
~A. How could compost bins reduce cafeteria trash during one semester?#Incorrect. This question needs research and analysis.
~B. How could later bus service affect student job access?#Incorrect. This question is researchable.
=C. What is the dictionary definition of compost?#Correct. This can be answered with one quick definition, so it is too narrow.
~D. How could a school garden support science learning?#Incorrect. This is a researchable question.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is biased?{
=A. Why are school gardens the best idea ever?#Correct. The phrase "best idea ever" assumes an answer before research begins.
~B. How could school gardens support science learning?#Incorrect. This is neutral and researchable.
~C. What maintenance challenges do school gardens create?#Incorrect. This is neutral and researchable.
~D. How could a school garden affect cafeteria taste tests?#Incorrect. This asks about an effect without biased wording.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Broad topic: library access. Which question best narrows the topic by audience?{
~A. Are libraries useful?#Incorrect. This is broad and does not name an audience.
~B. What is a library?#Incorrect. This is a definition question.
~C. Why do all people love libraries?#Incorrect. This is biased and too broad.
=D. How could evening library hours help students who work after school?#Correct. The audience is students who work after school.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research notes mention science lessons, cafeteria use, and maintenance problems for school gardens. What should a student do to refine the inquiry?{
~A. Study every possible school garden issue in one paragraph.#Incorrect. That scope is too large.
=B. Choose one focus, such as science learning or maintenance, and build the question around it.#Correct. A focused path makes the research manageable.
~C. Ignore the notes completely.#Incorrect. Inquiry should adjust based on findings.
~D. Turn the question into a personal preference.#Incorrect. Research should be evidence-based.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which phrase best limits the time frame of a research question?{
~A. "school gardens"#Incorrect. This is the topic, not a time frame.
~B. "for tenth-grade students"#Incorrect. This limits audience, not time.
=C. "during one semester"#Correct. This phrase limits the time period being studied.
~D. "science learning"#Incorrect. This limits subject area, not time.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Question: "How could a school recycling program reduce cafeteria waste during one semester?" Which part identifies the possible effect?{
~A. "school recycling program"#Incorrect. This names the solution or program.
~B. "during one semester"#Incorrect. This names the time frame.
~C. "How could"#Incorrect. This begins the question but does not name the effect.
=D. "reduce cafeteria waste"#Correct. This phrase names the effect the research will investigate.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Research note: "Garden programs can be difficult to maintain if no class, club, or staff member is responsible for watering and planning." Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
=A. A school garden may fail if no group is assigned to care for it.#Correct. This restates the note accurately.
~B. Gardens never need planning.#Incorrect. The note says planning is necessary.
~C. Watering is illegal in schools.#Incorrect. The note does not say this.
~D. Garden programs are always impossible.#Incorrect. The note describes a challenge, not impossibility.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is researchable because it requires evidence?{
~A. Is composting my favorite thing?#Incorrect. This asks for personal preference.
~B. What does compost mean?#Incorrect. This asks for a simple definition.
=C. How could composting affect the amount of cafeteria trash collected each week?#Correct. This question would need data about trash amounts.
~D. Why is composting obviously perfect?#Incorrect. This is biased.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A focused research question should usually avoid which feature?{
~A. A clear topic.#Incorrect. A clear topic is needed.
=B. A built-in opinion that decides the answer before research begins.#Correct. Research questions should remain open enough for evidence.
~C. A manageable scope.#Incorrect. Manageable scope is important.
~D. Evidence-based wording.#Incorrect. Evidence-based wording supports research.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Topic: student phone use. Which question has the best academic scope?{
=A. How could phone-free study periods affect homework completion for tenth-grade students during one grading period?#Correct. This is focused by action, effect, audience, and time.
~B. Are phones bad?#Incorrect. This is broad and opinion-based.
~C. What is a phone?#Incorrect. This is too narrow.
~D. Why should nobody ever use phones?#Incorrect. This is biased and extreme.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] What does "refine" mean in research?{
~A. To make a question more confusing.#Incorrect. Refinement should increase clarity.
~B. To remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Research depends on evidence.
~C. To change every topic into a personal opinion.#Incorrect. Research should avoid opinion-only wording.
=D. To make a question clearer, narrower, and more researchable.#Correct. Refining improves the question's focus.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is best for research after a student finds sources about bus schedules and student jobs?{
~A. Are buses vehicles?#Incorrect. This is too simple.
~B. Why are buses always the answer?#Incorrect. This is biased.
=C. How could later bus schedules affect after-school job access for students?#Correct. The question uses the source pattern to refine the inquiry.
~D. What color are buses?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and not connected to the research purpose.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question best narrows by location?{
~A. What is food waste?#Incorrect. This asks for a definition.
=B. How could a share table reduce unopened food waste in one middle school cafeteria?#Correct. The phrase "in one middle school cafeteria" narrows the location.
~C. Is food waste bad?#Incorrect. This is broad and opinion-based.
~D. Why does everyone waste food?#Incorrect. This is too broad and assumes too much.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A student's question is, "How can people fix pollution?" What is the best diagnosis?{
~A. It is too narrow because it asks for one fact.#Incorrect. The question is broad, not narrow.
~B. It is already focused by audience and place.#Incorrect. It does not name a specific audience or place.
=C. It is too broad because "people" and "pollution" cover too many places, causes, and solutions.#Correct. The scope is too large for one assignment.
~D. It cannot be improved.#Incorrect. It can be refined by narrowing the issue.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question best narrows pollution by solution and place?{
=A. How could reusable lunch trays reduce plastic waste in one school cafeteria?#Correct. The question names a solution and place.
~B. Is pollution bad everywhere?#Incorrect. This is broad and opinion-based.
~C. What is plastic?#Incorrect. This is too narrow.
~D. Why is every cafeteria wrong?#Incorrect. This is biased and not researchable.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail would help refine a question about school gardens?{
~A. A favorite color.#Incorrect. This does not help focus research.
=B. Whether the research will focus on science learning, food use, or maintenance.#Correct. Choosing one path narrows the scope.
~C. A random celebrity's birthday.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. The number of letters in the word garden.#Incorrect. This does not support inquiry.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is best if the assignment requires research from multiple sources?{
~A. What is the spelling of garden?#Incorrect. This does not require multiple sources.
~B. Do I like gardens?#Incorrect. This asks for personal opinion.
~C. Is a garden a garden?#Incorrect. This is not a meaningful research question.
=D. How could school gardens affect science learning and student participation?#Correct. This question requires evidence from more than one source.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence best explains why scope matters?{
=A. Scope keeps research manageable by showing what the project will and will not include.#Correct. Scope prevents the research from becoming too broad.
~B. Scope makes evidence unnecessary.#Incorrect. Research still requires evidence.
~C. Scope turns every question into a definition.#Incorrect. Scope limits the question; it does not reduce all research to definitions.
~D. Scope means the writer must avoid all focus.#Incorrect. Scope creates focus.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which research question is best after the student decides to focus on maintenance?{
~A. Why are school gardens amazing?#Incorrect. This is biased.
~B. What is soil?#Incorrect. This is a definition question.
=C. What maintenance plan would help a school garden remain usable during summer break?#Correct. This question focuses on maintenance and a specific time challenge.
~D. Should every school do everything?#Incorrect. This is too broad.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question best narrows by audience?{
~A. What is homework?#Incorrect. This is a definition.
~B. Why is homework terrible?#Incorrect. This is biased.
~C. Should people learn things?#Incorrect. This is too broad.
=D. How could a homework club support ninth-grade students who need a quiet study space?#Correct. The question names a specific student group.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which question is not researchable because it is mainly personal preference?{
~A. How could a pre-order form reduce unwanted lunch trays?#Incorrect. This can be researched.
=B. Do I personally like cafeteria recycling bins?#Correct. This asks for personal preference rather than evidence.
~C. What effect could later library hours have on student access?#Incorrect. This can be researched.
~D. How could a garden support biology observations?#Incorrect. This can be researched.
}



::ENG2_U04_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which checklist best matches a strong research question?{
=A. Focused topic, manageable scope, neutral wording, and evidence-based answer.#Correct. These features make a question researchable.
~B. Vague topic, biased wording, and no evidence needed.#Incorrect. This would weaken research.
~C. One-word topic only.#Incorrect. A topic is not a full research question.
~D. Personal preference with no sources.#Incorrect. Research requires evidence beyond preference.
}
